    "body": "Photo: VesselFinder\nBy Angelo Mathais India correspondent\n24 November 2026\nThe China-Persian Gulf corridor is poised for a container capacity boom as carriers eye sustained growth opportunities in an increasingly volatile trade environment, market updates suggest.\nCULines is reportedly preparing a major network expansion programme for the tradelane, riding on the strength of a strategic partnership recently concluded with port operator Gulftainer for priority operating rights at the UAE’s Khor Fakkan Port.\nAlongside Fujairah in the UAE and Sohar in Oman, Khor Fakkan has become a key Gulf bypass port, offering a lifeline of cargo flowing into the Middle East.\nSources say the Chinese carrier will deploy three large box ships from its fleet on the China-Gulf trade, marking a watershed moment in its service offerings.\n“Two ships similar to the 10,100 teu Express Berlin in size will be added,” one industry source told The Loadstar . The Danaos-owned vessel previously operated by Sealead has been chartered by CULInes.\n“Combined tonnage will be substantial for cargo connecting China and the Gulf,” the source added.\nEarlier this year, CULines launched the China-Gulf Express (CGX) service with a rotation of Qingdao-Ningbo-Shekou-Nansha-Port Klang-Khor Fakkan, on which the Express Berlin is currently deployed.\nKhor Fakkan has been operating under severe capacity strain, on- and off-dock, because of vessel call spikes tying up deployed industry capacity due to longer rotation cycles.\nVessel berthing/turnaround reliability through the priority handling partnership with Khor Fakkan operator Gulftainer is expected to fix those hurdles for CULines vessels calling at the contingency gateway, industry sources believe.\n“This partnership marks an important milestone for Khor Fakkan, with long-term volume commitments and strengthening its position as a strategic gateway connecting Asia to the UAE and the wider Gulf,” said Gulftainer. “Beyond port access, our shared ambition is to develop an integrated trade corridor, leveraging Gulftainer’s continued investments in Khor Fakkan, inland dry ports and logistics infrastructure across Sharjah.”\nCULines is not alone in rushing to field more capacity into the China-Khor Fakkan route.\nGulftainer’s container shipping arm, GT Lines, has also just set up a strategic vessel-sharing agreement with three budding Chinese container lines: Zhonggu Logistics; Sea Legend Shipping; and Ningbo Ocean Shipping.\nThe partners indicated they had plans to deploy five ships. two from GT Lines, to provide an upgraded Khor Fakkan-China Express 1 (KCX1) loop, with a 10-day sailing frequency they claimed would offer transit times of 18 days ex-South China, and 22 days ex-Central China, to Khor Fakkan.\n“Through Gulftainer’s integrated logistics ecosystem, we provide seamless onward connectivity to the UAE, GCC [Gulf Cooperation Council] and Iraq, leveraging from our Khor Fakkan-Sharjah corridor,” said GT Lines.\nThese strategic collaborations are especially significant because Khor Fakkan, Fujairah, and Sohar all continue to struggle with major delays and congestion, according to Kuehne + Nagel’s Seaexplorer platform, with vessels waiting for up to a week for a berth, while delays for inland container transfers can be even higher, often running up to 15 days.\nYou can contact the writer at [email protected]",
